<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F12.18" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Figure 12</span>: </span><span id="A1.F12.19" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Uncurated results for each dataset used in Tables <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Resolution usage ‣ 4 Progressive growing revisited ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
The images correspond to random outputs produced by our generator (config <span id="A1.F12.19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">f</span>), with truncation applied at all resolutions using <math id="A1.F12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras2018</a></cite>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F13" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/image_matrices/cat-cfg-a-seed9.jpg" id="A1.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" width="685" height="343" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="A1.F13.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="A1.F13.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:496.9pt;">Model 1: FID = 8.53, P = 0.64, R = 0.28, PPL = 924</span>
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/image_matrices/cat-cfg-f-snapshot27014-seed2.jpg" id="A1.F13.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="685" height="343" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.F13.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:496.9pt;">Model 2: FID = 8.53, P = 0.62, R = 0.29, PPL = 387</span>
<br class="ltx_break"></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F13.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 13</span>: </span><span id="A1.F13.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Uncurated examples from two generative models trained on <span id="A1.F13.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LSUN Cat</span> without truncation.
FID, precision, and recall are similar for models 1 and 2, even though the latter produces cat-shaped objects more often.
Perceptual path length (PPL) indicates a clear preference for model 2.
Model 1 corresponds to configuration <span id="A1.F13.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">a</span> in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Resolution usage ‣ 4 Progressive growing revisited ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and model 2 is an early training snapshot of configuration <span id="A1.F13.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">f</span>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F14" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/image_matrices/car-cfg-a-seed5.jpg" id="A1.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" width="685" height="367" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="A1.F14.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="A1.F14.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:496.9pt;">Model 1: FID = 3.27, P = 0.70, R = 0.44, PPL = 1485</span>
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/image_matrices/car-cfg-f-snapshot20153-seed6.jpg" id="A1.F14.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="685" height="367" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.F14.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:496.9pt;">Model 2: FID = 3.27, P = 0.67, R = 0.48, PPL = 437</span>
<br class="ltx_break"></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F14.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 14</span>: </span><span id="A1.F14.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Uncurated examples from two generative models trained on <span id="A1.F14.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LSUN Car</span> without truncation.
FID, precision, and recall are similar for models 1 and 2, even though the latter produces car-shaped objects more often.
Perceptual path length (PPL) indicates a clear preference for model 2.
Model 1 corresponds to configuration <span id="A1.F14.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">a</span> in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Resolution usage ‣ 4 Progressive growing revisited ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and model 2 is an early training snapshot of configuration <span id="A1.F14.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">f</span>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F15" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/missing_comparison_64.jpg" id="A1.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="134" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/missing_comparison_128.jpg" id="A1.F15.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="134" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/missing_comparison_256.jpg" id="A1.F15.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="134" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/missing_comparison_512.jpg" id="A1.F15.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="134" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/missing_comparison.jpg" id="A1.F15.g5" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="134" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/missing_64.jpg" id="A1.F15.g6" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="134" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/missing_128.jpg" id="A1.F15.g7" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="134" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/missing_256.jpg" id="A1.F15.g8" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="134" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/missing_512.jpg" id="A1.F15.g9" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="134" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/missing.jpg" id="A1.F15.g10" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="134" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="A1.F15.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="A1.F15.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="font-size:80%;width:96.9pt;">Feature map <math id="A1.F15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>64</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="A1.F15.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> <span id="A1.F15.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:96.9pt;">Feature map <math id="A1.F15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>128</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span> <span id="A1.F15.3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:96.9pt;">Feature map <math id="A1.F15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>256</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span> <span id="A1.F15.3.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:96.9pt;">Feature map <math id="A1.F15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>512</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>  <span id="A1.F15.3.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:96.9pt;">Generated image</span>
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F15.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Figure 15</span>: </span><span id="A1.F15.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">
An example of the importance of the droplet artifact in StyleGAN generator.
We compare two generated images, one successful and one severely corrupted.
The corresponding feature maps were normalized to the viewable dynamic range using instance normalization.
For the top image, the droplet artifact starts forming in <math id="A1.F15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>64</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> resolution, is clearly visible in <math id="A1.F15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>128</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and increasingly dominates the feature maps in higher resolutions.
For the bottom image, <math id="A1.F15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>64</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is qualitatively similar to the top row, but the droplet does not materialize in <math id="A1.F15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>128</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, the facial features are stronger in the normalized feature map.
This leads to an overshoot in <math id="A1.F15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>256</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by multiple spurious droplets forming in subsequent resolutions.
Based on our experience, it is rare that the droplet is missing from StyleGAN images, and indeed the generator fully relies on its existence.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F16" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/frequencies-PG.jpg" id="A1.F16.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="168" height="168" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/frequencies-PG-128.jpg" id="A1.F16.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="168" height="168" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/frequencies-new.jpg" id="A1.F16.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="168" height="168" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/artifacts/frequencies-new-128.jpg" id="A1.F16.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" width="168" height="168" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="A1.F16.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="A1.F16.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="font-size:80%;width:121.7pt;">Generated image</span><span id="A1.F16.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> <span id="A1.F16.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:121.7pt;">Feature map <math id="A1.F16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>128</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>  <span id="A1.F16.3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:121.7pt;">Generated image</span> <span id="A1.F16.3.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:121.7pt;">Feature map <math id="A1.F16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>128</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="A1.F16.4" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="A1.F16.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="font-size:90%;width:245.9pt;">(a) Progressive growing (config <span id="A1.F16.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">a</span>)</span><span id="A1.F16.4.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  <span id="A1.F16.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:245.9pt;">(b) Without progressive growing (config <span id="A1.F16.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">f</span>)</span>
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 16: </span>
Progressive growing leads to significantly higher frequency content in the intermediate layers. This compromises shift-invariance of the network and makes it harder to localize features precisely in the higher-resolution layers.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We include several large images that illustrate various aspects related to image quality.
Figure <a href="#A1.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix A Image quality ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> shows hand-picked examples illustrating the quality and diversity achievable using our method in <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FFHQ</span>, while
Figure <a href="#A1.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ Appendix A Image quality ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> shows uncurated results for all datasets mentioned in the paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#A1.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Appendix A Image quality ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and <a href="#A1.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ Appendix A Image quality ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> demonstrate cases where FID and P&amp;R give non-intuitive results, but PPL seems to be more in line with human judgement.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also include images relating to StyleGAN artifacts.
Figure <a href="#A1.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ Appendix A Image quality ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> shows a rare case where the blob artifact fails to appear in StyleGAN activations, leading to a seriously broken image.
Figure <a href="#A1.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ Appendix A Image quality ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> visualizes the activations inside Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> configurations <span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">a</span> and <span id="A1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">f</span>. It is evident that progressive growing leads to higher-frequency content in the intermediate layers, compromising shift invariance of the network. We hypothesize that this causes the observed uneven location preference for details when progressive growing is used.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Implementation details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implemented our techniques on top of the official TensorFlow implementation of StyleGAN<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="footnote5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/NVlabs/stylegan</span></span></span></span> corresponding to configuration <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">a</span> in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
We kept most of the details unchanged, including
the dimensionality of <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> (512),
mapping network architecture (8 fully connected layers, 100<math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> lower learning rate),
equalized learning rate for all trainable parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras2017</a></cite>,
leaky ReLU activation with <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.2</annotation></semantics></math>,
bilinear filtering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">zhang2019</a></cite> in all up/downsampling layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras2018</a></cite>,
minibatch standard deviation layer at the end of the discriminator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras2017</a></cite>,
exponential moving average of generator weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras2017</a></cite>,
style mixing regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras2018</a></cite>,
non-saturating logistic loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodfellow2014</a></cite> with <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mescheder2018</a></cite>,
Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">adam</a></cite> with the same hyperparameters (<math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0,\beta_{2}=0.99,\epsilon=10^{-8},\mathrm{minibatch}=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>minibatch</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0,\beta_{2}=0.99,\epsilon=10^{-8},\mathrm{minibatch}=32</annotation></semantics></math>),
and training datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">LSUN</a></cite>.
We performed all training runs on NVIDIA DGX-1 with 8 Tesla V100 GPUs using TensorFlow 1.14.0 and cuDNN 7.4.2.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generator redesign</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In configurations <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">b</span>–<span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">f</span> we replace the original StyleGAN generator with our revised architecture.
In addition to the changes highlighted in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we initialize components of the constant input <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and simplify the noise broadcast operations to use a single shared scaling factor for all feature maps.
Similar to Karras et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras2018</a></cite>, we initialize all weights using <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and all biases and noise scaling factors to zero, except for the biases of the affine transformation layers, which we initialize to one.
We employ weight modulation and demodulation in all convolution layers, except for the output layers (tRGB in Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.1 Alternative network architectures ‣ 4 Progressive growing revisited ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) where we leave out the demodulation.
With <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>1024</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> output resolution, the generator contains a total of 18 affine transformation layers where the first one corresponds to <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> resolution, the next two correspond to <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>8</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so forth.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Weight demodulation</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Considering the practical implementation of Equations <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, it is important to note that the resulting set of weights will be different for each sample in a minibatch, which rules out direct implementation using standard convolution primitives.
Instead, we choose to employ <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grouped convolutions</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">krizhevsky2012</a></cite> that were originally proposed as a way to reduce computational costs by dividing the input feature maps into multiple independent groups, each with their own dedicated set of weights.
We implement Equations <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> by temporarily reshaping the weights and activations so that each convolution sees one sample with <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> groups — instead of <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samples with one group.
This approach is highly efficient because the reshaping operations do not actually modify the contents of the weight and activation tensors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Lazy regularization</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In configurations <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">c</span>–<span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">f</span> we employ lazy regularization (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Lazy regularization ‣ 3 Image quality and generator smoothness ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) by evaluating the regularization terms (<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and path length) in a separate regularization pass that we execute once every <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> training iterations.
We share the internal state of the Adam optimizer between the main loss and the regularization terms, so that the optimizer first sees gradients from the main loss for <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, followed by gradients from the regularization terms for one iteration.
To compensate for the fact that we now perform <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> training iterations instead of <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we adjust the optimizer hyperparameters <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}=c\cdot\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}=c\cdot\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}^{\prime}=(\beta_{1})^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}^{\prime}=(\beta_{1})^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}^{\prime}=(\beta_{2})^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}^{\prime}=(\beta_{2})^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=k/(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=k/(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also multiply the regularization term by <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to balance the overall magnitude of its gradients.
We use <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=16</annotation></semantics></math> for the discriminator and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math> for the generator.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Path length regularization</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Configurations <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">d</span>–<span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">f</span> include our new path length regularizer (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Path length regularization ‣ 3 Image quality and generator smoothness ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
We initialize the target scale <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> to zero and track it on a per-GPU basis as the exponential moving average of <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\mathbf{J}_{{\bf w}}^{T}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\mathbf{J}_{{\bf w}}^{T}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> using decay coefficient <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{pl}}=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>pl</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{pl}}=0.99</annotation></semantics></math>.
We weight our regularization term by</p>
<table id="A2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\textrm{pl}}=\frac{\ln 2}{r^{2}(\ln r-\ln 2)}\textrm{,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext>pl</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\textrm{pl}}=\frac{\ln 2}{r^{2}(\ln r-\ln 2)}\textrm{,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the output resolution (e.g. <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1024</annotation></semantics></math>).
We have found these parameter choices to work reliably across all configurations and datasets.
To ensure that our regularizer interacts correctly with style mixing regularization, we compute it as an average of all individual layers of the synthesis network. Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Effects of path length regularization ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> provides detailed analysis of the effects of our regularizer on the mapping between <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and image space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Progressive growing</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In configurations <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">a</span>–<span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">d</span> we use progressive growing with the same parameters as Karras et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras2018</a></cite> (start at <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>8</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> resolution and learning rate <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, train for 600k images per resolution, fade in next resolution for 600k images, increase learning rate gradually by <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times</annotation></semantics></math>).
In configurations <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">e</span>–<span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">f</span> we disable progressive growing and set the learning rate to a fixed value <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=2\cdot 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=2\cdot 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we found to provide the best results.
In addition, we use output skips in the generator and residual connections in the discriminator as detailed in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Alternative network architectures ‣ 4 Progressive growing revisited ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset-specific tuning</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to Karras et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras2018</a></cite>, we augment the <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FFHQ</span> dataset with horizontal flips to effectively increase the number of training images from 70k to 140k, and we do not perform any augmentation for the <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LSUN</span> datasets.
We have found that the optimal choices for the training length and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization weight <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> tend to vary considerably between datasets and configurations.
We use <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=10</annotation></semantics></math> for all training runs except for configuration <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">e</span> in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, as well as <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LSUN Church</span> and <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LSUN Horse</span> in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Resolution usage ‣ 4 Progressive growing revisited ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where we use <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=100</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is possible that further tuning of <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> could provide additional benefits.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Performance optimizations</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We profiled our training runs extensively and found that — in our case — the default primitives for image filtering, up/downsampling, bias addition, and leaky ReLU had surprisingly high overheads in terms of training time and GPU memory footprint.
This motivated us to optimize these operations using hand-written CUDA kernels.
We implemented filtered up/downsampling as a single fused operation, and bias and activation as another one.
In configuration <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">e</span> at <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>1024</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> resolution, our optimizations improved the overall training time by about 30% and memory footprint by about 20%.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Effects of path length regularization</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The path length regularizer described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Path length regularization ‣ 3 Image quality and generator smoothness ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> is of the form:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pl}}=\mathbb{E}_{\bf w}\mathbb{E}_{\bf y}\left(\left\lVert\mathbf{J}_{{\bf w}}^{T}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>pl</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pl}}=\mathbb{E}_{\bf w}\mathbb{E}_{\bf y}\left(\left\lVert\mathbf{J}_{{\bf w}}^{T}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bf y}\in\real^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf y}\in\real^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a unit normal distributed random variable in the space of generated images (of dimension <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=3wh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=3wh</annotation></semantics></math>, namely the RGB image dimensions), <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bf J}_{\bf w}\in\real^{M\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf J}_{\bf w}\in\real^{M\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Jacobian matrix of the generator function <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g:\real^{L}\mapsto\real^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\real^{L}\mapsto\real^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> at a latent space point <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bf w}\in\real^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}\in\real^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a global value that expresses the desired scale of the gradients.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Effect on pointwise Jacobians</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The value of this prior is minimized when the inner expectation over <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math> is minimized at every latent space point <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}</annotation></semantics></math> separately. In this subsection, we show that the inner expectation is (approximately) minimized when the Jacobian matrix <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf J}_{\bf w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf J}_{\bf w}</annotation></semantics></math> is orthogonal, up to a global scaling factor. The general strategy is to use the well-known fact that, in high dimensions <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the density of a unit normal distribution is concentrated on a spherical shell of radius <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inner expectation is then minimized when the matrix <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf J}^{T}_{\bf w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf J}^{T}_{\bf w}</annotation></semantics></math> scales the function under expectation to have its minima at this radius. This is achieved by any orthogonal matrix (with suitable global scale that is the same at every <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by considering the inner expectation</p>
<table id="A3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}:=\mathbb{E}_{\bf y}\left(\left\lVert{\bf J}^{T}_{\bf w}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}:=\mathbb{E}_{\bf y}\left(\left\lVert{\bf J}^{T}_{\bf w}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We first note that the radial symmetry of the distribution of <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as of the <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm, allows us to focus on diagonal matrices only. This is seen using the Singular Value Decomposition <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf J}^{T}_{\bf w}=\mathbf{U}\tilde{\bf\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf J}^{T}_{\bf w}=\mathbf{U}\tilde{\bf\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{U}\in\real^{L\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\in\real^{L\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{V}\in\real^{M\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}\in\real^{M\times M}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthogonal matrices, and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bf\Sigma}=[{\bf\Sigma}~\mathbf{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bf\Sigma}=[{\bf\Sigma}~\mathbf{0}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a horizontal concatenation of a diagonal matrix <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bf\Sigma}\in\real^{L\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf\Sigma}\in\real^{L\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> and a zero matrix <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{0}\in\real^{L\times(M-L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{0}\in\real^{L\times(M-L)}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Golub2013</a></cite>. Because rotating a unit normal random variable by an orthogonal matrix leaves the distribution unchanged, and rotating a vector leaves its norm unchanged, the expression simplifies to</p>
<table id="A3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\bf y}\left(\left\lVert\mathbf{U}\tilde{\bf\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{T}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\bf y}\left(\left\lVert\mathbf{U}\tilde{\bf\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{T}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\bf y}\left(\left\lVert\tilde{\bf\Sigma}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\bf y}\left(\left\lVert\tilde{\bf\Sigma}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the zero matrix in <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bf\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bf\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> drops the dimensions of <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math> beyond <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively marginalizing its distribution over those dimensions. The marginalized distribution is again a unit normal distribution over the remaining <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions. We are then left to consider the minimization of the expression</p>
<table id="A3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}=\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{\bf y}}\left(\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\tilde{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}=\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{\bf y}}\left(\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\tilde{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">over diagonal square matrices <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bf\Sigma}\in\real^{L\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf\Sigma}\in\real^{L\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math> is unit normal distributed in dimension <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. To summarize, all matrices <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf J}^{T}_{\bf w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf J}^{T}_{\bf w}</annotation></semantics></math> that share the same singular values with <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> produce the same value for the original loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we show that this expression is minimized when the diagonal matrix <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> has a specific identical value at every diagonal entry, i.e., it is a constant multiple of an identity matrix. We first write the expectation as an integral over the probability density of <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int\left(\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\tilde{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}p_{\tilde{\bf y}}(\tilde{\bf y})~\mathrm{d}\tilde{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int\left(\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\tilde{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}p_{\tilde{\bf y}}(\tilde{\bf y})~\mathrm{d}\tilde{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(2\pi)^{-\frac{L}{2}}\int\left(\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\tilde{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{\tilde{\bf y}^{T}\tilde{\bf y}}{2}\right)~\mathrm{d}\tilde{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(2\pi)^{-\frac{L}{2}}\int\left(\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\tilde{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{\tilde{\bf y}^{T}\tilde{\bf y}}{2}\right)~\mathrm{d}\tilde{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Observing the radially symmetric form of the density, we change into a polar coordinates <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bf y}=r\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bf y}=r\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r\in\real_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\real_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distance from origin, and <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathbb{S}^{L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathbb{S}^{L-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a unit vector, i.e., a point on the <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-1</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional unit sphere. This change of variables introduces a Jacobian factor <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{L-1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{{\bf w}}=(2\pi)^{-\frac{L}{2}}\int_{\mathbb{S}}\int_{0}^{\infty}\left(r\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\phi\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}r^{L-1}\\
\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{r^{2}}{2}\right)~\mathrm{d}r~\mathrm{d}\phi" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi>𝕊</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{{\bf w}}=(2\pi)^{-\frac{L}{2}}\int_{\mathbb{S}}\int_{0}^{\infty}\left(r\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\phi\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}r^{L-1}\\
\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{r^{2}}{2}\right)~\mathrm{d}r~\mathrm{d}\phi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The probability density <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(2\pi)^{-L/2}r^{L-1}\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{r^{2}}{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\pi)^{-L/2}r^{L-1}\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{r^{2}}{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is then an <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional unit normal density expressed in polar coordinates, dependent only on the radius and not on the angle. A standard argument by Taylor approximation shows that when <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is high, for any <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> the density is well approximated by density <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(2\pi e/L)^{-L/2}\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{1}{2}(r-\mu)^{2}/\sigma^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\pi e/L)^{-L/2}\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{1}{2}(r-\mu)^{2}/\sigma^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a (unnormalized) one-dimensional normal density in <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, centered at <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\sqrt{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\sqrt{L}</annotation></semantics></math> of standard deviation <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1/\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1/\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bishop2006</a></cite>. In other words, the density of the <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional unit normal distribution is concentrated on a shell of radius <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting this density into the integral, the loss becomes approximately</p>
<table id="A3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}\approx(2\pi e/L)^{-L/2}\int_{\mathbb{S}}\int_{0}^{\infty}\left(r\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\phi\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}\\
\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{\left(r-\sqrt{L}\right)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)~\mathrm{d}r~\mathrm{d}\phi," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕊</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>‖</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}\approx(2\pi e/L)^{-L/2}\int_{\mathbb{S}}\int_{0}^{\infty}\left(r\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\phi\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}\\
\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{\left(r-\sqrt{L}\right)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)~\mathrm{d}r~\mathrm{d}\phi,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where the approximation becomes exact in the limit of infinite dimension <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To minimize this loss, we set <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the function <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(r\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\phi\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(r\left\lVert{\bf\Sigma}\phi\right\rVert_{2}-a\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> obtains minimal values on the spherical shell of radius <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is achieved by <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf\Sigma}=\frac{a}{\sqrt{L}}\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf\Sigma}=\frac{a}{\sqrt{L}}\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereby the function becomes constant in <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and the expression reduces to</p>
<table id="A3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}\approx(2\pi e/L)^{-L/2}\mathcal{A}(\mathbb{S})a^{2}L^{-1}\int_{0}^{\infty}\left(r-\sqrt{L}\right)^{2}\\
\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{\left(r-\sqrt{L}\right)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)~\mathrm{d}r," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>L</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{{\bf w}}\approx(2\pi e/L)^{-L/2}\mathcal{A}(\mathbb{S})a^{2}L^{-1}\int_{0}^{\infty}\left(r-\sqrt{L}\right)^{2}\\
\mathrm{exp}\left(-\frac{\left(r-\sqrt{L}\right)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)~\mathrm{d}r,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\mathbb{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\mathbb{S})</annotation></semantics></math> is the surface area of the unit sphere (and like the other constant factors, irrelevant for minimization). Note that the zero of the parabola <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r-\sqrt{L})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r-\sqrt{L})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the maximum of the probability density, and therefore this choice of <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes the inner integral in Eq. <a href="#A3.E7" title="In C.1 Effect on pointwise Jacobians ‣ Appendix C Effects of path length regularization ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> separately for every <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, we have shown that — assuming a high dimensionality <math id="A3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> of the latent space — the value of the path length prior (Eq. <a href="#A3.E6" title="In Appendix C Effects of path length regularization ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is minimized when all singular values of the Jacobian matrix of the generator are equal to a global constant, at every latent space point <math id="A3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., they are orthogonal up to a globally constant scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">While in theory <math id="A3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> merely scales the values of the mapping without changing its properties and could be set to a fixed value (e.g., <math id="A3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>), in practice it does affect the dynamics of the training. If the imposed scale does not match the scale induced by the random initialization of the network, the training spends its critical early steps in pushing the weights towards the required overall magnitudes, rather than enforcing the actual objective of interest. This may degrade the internal state of the network weights and lead to sub-optimal performance in later training. Empirically we find that setting a fixed scale reduces the consistency of the training results across training runs and datasets. Instead, we set <math id="A3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> dynamically based on a running average of the existing scale of the Jacobians, namely <math id="A3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\approx\mathbb{E}_{{\bf w},{\bf y}}\left(\left\lVert{\bf J}^{T}_{w}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\approx\mathbb{E}_{{\bf w},{\bf y}}\left(\left\lVert{\bf J}^{T}_{w}{\bf y}\right\rVert_{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. With this choice the prior targets the scale of the local Jacobians towards whatever global average already exists, rather than forcing a specific global average. This also eliminates the need to measure the appropriate scale of the Jacobians explicitly, as is done by Odena et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Odena2018</a></cite> who consider a related conditioning prior.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F17" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="/html/1912.04958/assets/jacobian_ffhq.svg" id="A3.F17.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" width="336" height="252"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="/html/1912.04958/assets/jacobian_car.svg" id="A3.F17.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" width="336" height="252"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F17.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 17</span>: </span><span id="A3.F17.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
The mean and standard deviation of the magnitudes of sorted singular values of the Jacobian matrix evaluated at random latent space points <math id="A3.F17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}</annotation></semantics></math>, with largest eigenvalue normalized to <math id="A3.F17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. In both datasets, path length regularization (Config D) and novel architecture (Config F) exhibit better conditioning; notably, the effect is more pronounced in the Cars dataset that contains much more variability, and where path length regularization has a relatively stronger effect on the PPL metric (Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ C.1 Effect on pointwise Jacobians ‣ Appendix C Effects of path length regularization ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> shows empirically measured magnitudes of singular values of the Jacobian matrix for networks trained with and without path length regularization. While orthogonality is not reached, the eigenvalues of the regularized network are closer to one another, implying better conditioning, with the strength of the effect correlated with the PPL metric (Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Effect on global properties of generator mapping</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous subsection, we found that the prior encourages the Jacobians of the generator mapping to be everywhere orthogonal. While Figure <a href="#A3.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ C.1 Effect on pointwise Jacobians ‣ Appendix C Effects of path length regularization ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> shows that the mapping does not satisfy this constraint exactly in practice, it is instructive to consider what global properties the constraint implies for mappings that do. Without loss of generality, we assume unit global scale for the matrices to simplify the presentation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The key property is that that a mapping <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g:\real^{L}\mapsto\real^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\real^{L}\mapsto\real^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with everywhere orthogonal Jacobians preserves the lengths of curves. To see this, let <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u:[t_{0},t_{1}]\mapsto\real^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:[t_{0},t_{1}]\mapsto\real^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrize a curve in the latent space. Mapping the curve through the generator <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a curve <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}=g\circ u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}=g\circ u</annotation></semantics></math> in the space of images. Its arc length is</p>
<table id="A3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}\lvert\tilde{u}^{\prime}(t)\rvert~\mathrm{d}t," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}\lvert\tilde{u}^{\prime}(t)\rvert~\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where prime denotes derivative with respect to <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. By chain rule, this equals</p>
<table id="A3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}\lvert J_{g}(u(t))u^{\prime}(t)\rvert~\mathrm{d}t," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}\lvert J_{g}(u(t))u^{\prime}(t)\rvert~\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J_{g}\in\real^{L\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{g}\in\real^{L\times M}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Jacobian matrix of <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated at <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. By our assumption, the Jacobian is orthogonal, and consequently it leaves the 2-norm of the vector <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> unaffected:</p>
<table id="A3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}\lvert u^{\prime}(t)\rvert~\mathrm{d}t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}\lvert u^{\prime}(t)\rvert~\mathrm{d}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">This is the length of the curve <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> in the latent space, prior to mapping with <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the lengths of <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}</annotation></semantics></math> are equal, and so <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the length of any curve.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the language of differential geometry, <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> isometrically embeds the Euclidean latent space <sup id="A3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">L</span></sup> into a submanifold <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in <sup id="A3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">M</span></sup> — e.g., the manifold of images representing faces, embedded within the space of all possible RGB images. A consequence of isometry is that straight line segments in the latent space are mapped to geodesics, or shortest paths, on the image manifold: a straight line <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> that connects two latent space points cannot be made any shorter, so neither can there be a shorter on-manifold image-space path between the corresponding images than <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\circ v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\circ v</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, a geodesic on the manifold of face images is a continuous morph between two faces that incurs the minimum total amount of change (as measured by <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> difference in RGB space) when one sums up the image difference in each step of the morph.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Isometry is not achieved in practice, as demonstrated in empirical experiments in the previous subsection. The full loss function of the training is a combination of potentially conflicting criteria, and it is not clear if a genuinely isometric mapping would be capable of expressing the image manifold of interest. Nevertheless, a pressure to make the mapping as isometric as possible has desirable consequences. In particular, it discourages unnecessary “detours”: in a non-constrained generator mapping, a latent space interpolation between two similar images may pass through any number of distant images in RGB space. With regularization, the mapping is encouraged to place distant images in different regions of the latent space, so as to obtain short image paths between any two endpoints.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Projection method details</h2>

<figure id="A4.F18" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A4.F18.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.F18.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="font-size:80%;width:247.6pt;">Generated target image</span><span id="A4.F18.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> <span id="A4.F18.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:247.6pt;">Real target image</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/cropa-fakenoreg.jpg" id="A4.F18.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="342" height="256" alt="Refer to caption"> <img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/cropa-realnoreg.jpg" id="A4.F18.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="342" height="256" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/cropb-fakenoreg.jpg" id="A4.F18.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"> <img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/cropb-fake--reg.jpg" id="A4.F18.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"> <img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/cropb-realnoreg.jpg" id="A4.F18.g5" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"> <img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/cropb-real--reg.jpg" id="A4.F18.g6" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/crop-fakenoreg-noise0008.jpg" id="A4.F18.g7" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"> <img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/crop-fake--reg-noise0008.jpg" id="A4.F18.g8" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"> <img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/crop-realnoreg-noise0008.jpg" id="A4.F18.g9" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"> <img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/crop-real--reg-noise0008.jpg" id="A4.F18.g10" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break"><img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/crop-fakenoreg-noise0012.jpg" id="A4.F18.g11" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"> <img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/crop-fake--reg-noise0012.jpg" id="A4.F18.g12" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"> <img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/crop-realnoreg-noise0012.jpg" id="A4.F18.g13" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption"> <img src="/html/1912.04958/assets/figures/noisereg_car/crop-real--reg-noise0012.jpg" id="A4.F18.g14" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="170" height="128" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A4.F18.3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:123.0pt;">No noise</span> <span id="A4.F18.3.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:123.0pt;">With noise</span> <span id="A4.F18.3.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:123.0pt;">No noise</span> <span id="A4.F18.3.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:123.0pt;">With noise</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A4.F18.3.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:123.0pt;">regularization</span> <span id="A4.F18.3.2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:123.0pt;">regularization</span> <span id="A4.F18.3.2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:123.0pt;">regularization</span> <span id="A4.F18.3.2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:123.0pt;">regularization</span>
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F18.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Figure 18</span>: </span><span id="A4.F18.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Effect of noise regularization in latent-space projection where we also optimize the contents of the noise inputs of the synthesis network.
Top to bottom: target image, re-synthesized image, contents of two noise maps at different resolutions.
When regularization is turned off in this test, we only normalize the noise maps to zero mean and unit variance, which leads the optimization to sneak signal into the noise maps.
Enabling the noise regularization prevents this. The model used here corresponds to configuration <span id="A4.F18.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">f</span> in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Instance normalization revisited ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a target image <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we seek to find the corresponding <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf w}\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and per-layer noise maps denoted <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}_{i}\in\real^{r_{i}\times r_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒏</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}_{i}\in\real^{r_{i}\times r_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is the layer index and <math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the resolution of the <math id="A4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th noise map.
The baseline StyleGAN generator in 1024<math id="A4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>1024 resolution has 18 noise inputs, i.e., two for each resolution from 4<math id="A4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>4 to 1024<math id="A4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>1024 pixels.
Our improved architecture has one fewer noise input because we do not add noise to the learned 4<math id="A4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>4 constant (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Removing normalization artifacts ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Before optimization, we compute <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\bf w}=\mathbb{E}_{\bf z}\,f({\bf z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\bf w}=\mathbb{E}_{\bf z}\,f({\bf z})</annotation></semantics></math> by running 10 000 random latent codes <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf z}</annotation></semantics></math> through the mapping network <math id="A4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also approximate the scale of <math id="A4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> by computing <math id="A4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\bf w}=\mathbb{E}_{\bf z}\,\lVert f({\bf z})-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\bf w}\rVert^{2}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\bf w}=\mathbb{E}_{\bf z}\,\lVert f({\bf z})-\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\bf w}\rVert^{2}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the average square Euclidean distance to the center.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At the beginning of optimization, we initialize <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf w}=\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\bf w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}=\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\bf w}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}_{i}=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}_{i}=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
The trainable parameters are the components of <math id="A4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as all components in all noise maps <math id="A4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The optimization is run for 1000 iterations using Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">adam</a></cite> with default parameters.
Maximum learning rate is <math id="A4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathit{max}}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝑚𝑎𝑥</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathit{max}}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is ramped up from zero linearly during the first 50 iterations and ramped down to zero using a cosine schedule during the last 250 iterations.
In the first three quarters of the optimization we add Gaussian noise to <math id="A4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}</annotation></semantics></math> when evaluating the loss function
as <math id="A4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf\tilde{w}}={\bf w}+\mathcal{N}(0,0.05\,\sigma_{\bf w}t^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf\tilde{w}}={\bf w}+\mathcal{N}(0,0.05\,\sigma_{\bf w}t^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> goes from one to zero during the first 750 iterations.
This adds stochasticity to the optimization and stabilizes finding of the global optimum.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given that we are explicitly optimizing the noise maps, we must be careful to avoid the optimization from sneaking actual signal into them.
Thus we include several noise map regularization terms in our loss function, in addition to an image quality term.
The image quality term is the LPIPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang2018metric</a></cite> distance between target image <math id="A4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and the synthesized image:
<math id="A4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathit{image}}=D_{\mathrm{LPIPS}}[\boldsymbol{x},g({\bf\tilde{w}},\boldsymbol{n}_{0},\boldsymbol{n}_{1},\ldots)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝑖𝑚𝑎𝑔𝑒</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>LPIPS</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathit{image}}=D_{\mathrm{LPIPS}}[\boldsymbol{x},g({\bf\tilde{w}},\boldsymbol{n}_{0},\boldsymbol{n}_{1},\ldots)]</annotation></semantics></math>.
For increased performance and stability, we downsample both images to 256<math id="A4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>256 resolution before computing the LPIPS distance.
Regularization of the noise maps is performed on multiple resolution scales.
For this purpose, we form for each noise map greater than 8<math id="A4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>8 in size a pyramid down to 8<math id="A4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>8 resolution by averaging 2<math id="A4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>2 pixel neighborhoods and multiplying by 2 at each step to retain the expected unit variance.
These downsampled noise maps are used for regularization only and have no part in synthesis.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let us denote the original noise maps by <math id="A4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}_{i,0}=\boldsymbol{n}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}_{i,0}=\boldsymbol{n}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the downsampled versions by <math id="A4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, let <math id="A4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the resolution of an original (<math id="A4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0</annotation></semantics></math>) or downsampled (<math id="A4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) noise map so that <math id="A4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,j+1}=r_{i,j}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,j+1}=r_{i,j}/2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The regularization term for noise map <math id="A4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is then</p>
<table id="A4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\frac{1}{r_{i,j}^{2}}\cdot\sum_{x,y}\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j}(x,y)\cdot\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j}(x-1,y)\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\frac{1}{r_{i,j}^{2}}\cdot\sum_{x,y}\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j}(x,y)\cdot\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j}(x-1,y)\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\frac{1}{r_{i,j}^{2}}\cdot\sum_{x,y}\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j}(x,y)\cdot\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j}(x,y-1)\right)^{2}\textrm{,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒏</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>,</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\frac{1}{r_{i,j}^{2}}\cdot\sum_{x,y}\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j}(x,y)\cdot\boldsymbol{n}_{i,j}(x,y-1)\right)^{2}\textrm{,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where the noise map is considered to wrap at the edges.
The regularization term is thus sum of squares of the resolution-normalized autocorrelation coefficients at one pixel shifts horizontally and vertically, which should be zero for a normally distributed signal.
The overall loss term is then <math id="A4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathit{total}}=L_{\mathit{image}}+\alpha\sum_{i,j}L_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑡𝑎𝑙</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝑖𝑚𝑎𝑔𝑒</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathit{total}}=L_{\mathit{image}}+\alpha\sum_{i,j}L_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. In all our tests, we have used noise regularization weight <math id="A4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In addition, we renormalize all noise maps to zero mean and unit variance after each optimization step. Figure <a href="#A4.F18" title="Figure 18 ‣ Appendix D Projection method details ‣ Analyzing and Improving the Image Quality of StyleGAN" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> illustrates the effect of noise regularization on the resulting noise maps.</p>
</div>
</section>
